<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Arjunayanas</h1><p class="st-meta">100 BCE – 350 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small republic of north-eastern Rajasthan, known from its coins, which claimed descent from the epic hero Arjuna.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Arjunayanas are known mainly from coins and from one inscription. They took their name from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arjuna">Arjuna</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandavas">Pandava</a> hero of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>, and appear to have emerged as an independent gana in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bharatpur-state">Bharatpur</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/alwar-state">Alwar</a> region after the fall of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> coins, dated to around the 1st century BCE, bear the legend Arjunayananam jayah, victory of the Arjunayanas, which shows they were governed as a republic rather than by a king. Nothing more is known of their society.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 4th century the Arjunayanas were among the peoples that submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samudragupta">Samudragupta</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a>, according to his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Allahabad</a> pillar inscription, after which they disappear from the record.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/arjunayanas.jpg" alt="Arjunayanas" width="320" height="384"><figcaption>Image: Uwe Dedering · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indian_tribes_between_the_Indus_and_the_Ganges.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>100 BCE – 350 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Prakrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Arjunayana Gana</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=125&amp;focus=arjunayanas" aria-label="Arjunayanas on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6808 1348 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Arjunayanas lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7362 1550h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7128 1604h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7128 1604h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day India. <a href="/atlas?year=125&amp;focus=arjunayanas">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

